From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Divy Srivastava <divy@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Forward ThinStrings shortcut by the scavenger

When the scavenger skips over a ThinString, it points the current slot at the
internalized string but leaves no forwarding address. Weak handles are checked
after the scavenge and only look at the forwarding address, so a weak handle to
a ThinString is cleared while its string is still alive.

Mark-compact and the scavenger's own ConsString shortcut both write the
forwarding address. Do the same here.

diff --git a/src/heap/scavenger.cc b/src/heap/scavenger.cc
index de706edff12eeb9942695c461af563652add1c99..1f6e1f6d5514d5616ef7349c57e20e56154b7772 100644
--- a/src/heap/scavenger.cc
+++ b/src/heap/scavenger.cc
@@ -2157,14 +2157,16 @@ SlotCallbackResult Scavenger::EvacuateThinString(
                     std::is_same_v<THeapObjectSlot, HeapObjectSlot>,
                 "Only FullHeapObjectSlot and HeapObjectSlot are expected here");
   if (shortcut_strings_) {
-    // The ThinString should die after Scavenge, so avoid writing the proper
-    // forwarding pointer and instead just signal the actual object as forwarded
-    // reference.
+    // The ThinString dies after Scavenge, so point the slot at the actual
+    // string.
     Tagged<String> actual = object->actual();
     // ThinStrings always refer to internalized strings, which are always in old
     // space.
     DCHECK(!HeapLayout::InYoungGeneration(actual));
     UpdateHeapObjectReferenceSlot(slot, actual);
+    // Record the forwarding address too, so weak handles processed after the
+    // scavenge are updated to the actual string instead of cleared.
+    object->set_map_word_forwarded(actual, kRelaxedStore);
     return REMOVE_SLOT;
   }
 
